#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Managed headless Chromium for one sandbox (browser contract v1).

One browser serves the whole sandbox: every member of the group (the chat
and its Forks) shares it, its tabs and its sign-ins, as they share
/workspace. It runs as its own user (``agent-browser``), which no member's
turn-end reap scans, so it outlives the turn and the member that started it.

The platform starts the service once per container start, as that user::

    cloud-agent-browser service start

It serves CDP on loopback straight away and starts Chromium only when the
first client connects; with no client for the idle timeout Chromium stops
again and the service waits for the next one. A member's commands:

- ``start`` makes sure Chromium runs and prints a WebSocket URL that any CDP
  client (Playwright's ``connectOverCDP``, Playwright MCP ``--cdp-endpoint``,
  Chrome DevTools MCP ``--wsEndpoint``, Puppeteer ``browserWSEndpoint``) can
  connect to. There is deliberately no HTTP discovery endpoint
  (``/json/version``): clients resolve it against the server's root, which
  would drop the token from the path.
- ``status``, ``stop`` (closes Chromium for every member; the profile stays)
  and ``self-test`` do what they say.

Why a daemon in front of Chromium instead of ``--remote-debugging-port``:
Chromium's own debug port has no authentication, and anything on the
container's loopback could drive it. So Chromium speaks CDP only over
``--remote-debugging-pipe`` to this process, which serves it on loopback
behind a random path token. The token is in a file the service user owns and
only the sandbox group can read.

A pipe is a single CDP connection, but several tools may want the browser at
once. Each client therefore gets its own browser-level session
(``Target.attachToBrowserTarget``); its commands are sent on that session with
ids rewritten into one space, and every reply and event is routed back to the
client that owns the session it came from. A client never sees another
client's sessions. ``Browser.close`` from a client ends only that client's
connection: the browser belongs to the sandbox, not to one tool.

It runs isolated (``-I``), like the other helpers in this image, and uses the
standard library only.
"""

